<header class="r-masthead">
    <span class="r-eyebrow">From the Oven &middot; No. II &middot; Summer</span>
    <h1 class="r-title">The <em>Cherry Pie</em><br>That Started It All</h1>
    <p class="r-lede">The first pie I photographed for the Ledger &mdash; and the reason this chapter exists.</p>
    <div class="r-meta"><div><span class="l">Makes</span><span class="v">one 9-inch pie</span></div><div><span class="l">Oven</span><span class="v">400&deg; then 350&deg;F</span></div><div><span class="l">Time</span><span class="v">1 hour + cooling</span></div></div>
  </header>

  <section class="r-plate">
    <figure>
      <img src="../images/cherry-pie.jpg" alt="A slice of cherry pie on a painted plate, fresh cherries scattered around">
      <figcaption>The slice that began the chapter</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <div class="r-body"><div class="r-body-inner"><div class="r-headnote">
    <p>Tart cherries, a whisper of almond, an afternoon with nowhere else to be. It is not a difficult pie, only an unhurried one &mdash; the kind of thing worth doing on a quiet day. Let it cool completely before you cut it. A warm cherry pie is a beautiful, impatient mess; a cooled one slices clean.</p>
  </div></div></div>

  <div class="r-aside"><p>Begins with the house <a href="pie-crust.html" style="color:#6B5C3E;border-bottom:1px solid #C9C1B4;text-decoration:none">Pie Crust</a> &mdash; made and chilled ahead.</p></div>

  <div class="r-sec">
    <div class="r-sec-label">Ingredients</div>
    <div class="r-group"><p class="r-group-label">For the filling</p><ul class="r-ing"><li>5 cups tart cherries (fresh or frozen)</li><li>&frac34; cup sugar (up to 1 cup for a sweeter pie)</li><li>3 Tbsp cornstarch</li><li>1 Tbsp lemon juice</li><li>&frac12; tsp almond extract</li><li>1 Tbsp butter, for dotting</li><li>1 egg, beaten, for the wash</li></ul></div>
  </div>

  <div class="r-sec">
    <div class="r-sec-label">Method</div>
    <ol class="r-steps"><li>Roll out the bottom crust and settle it into the pie dish.</li><li>Combine the cherries, sugar, cornstarch, lemon juice, and almond extract.</li><li>Pour the filling into the crust.</li><li>Dot the top with butter.</li><li>Roll out the top crust, lay it over the filling, trim, and crimp the edges.</li><li>Cut a few steam vents, then brush with the egg wash.</li><li>Bake at 400&deg;F for 20 minutes.</li><li>Reduce to 350&deg;F and bake 35&ndash;40 minutes more, until the juices bubble thickly through the vents.</li></ol>
  </div>

  <section class="r-plate">
    <figure>
      <img src="../images/cherry-pie-alamode.jpg" alt="A slice of cherry pie served with a scoop of ice cream">
      <figcaption>Cooled, then served slowly</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="r-note"><p>"Let it cool completely before slicing. The almond is quiet but it's the whole secret &mdash; don't leave it out."</p></section>

